[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: A reserved field name can be declared with a type that is then ignored

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-RESERVED-FIELD-DECLARATION-LIES
TITLE: A reserved field name can be declared with a type that is then ignored
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Declaring UID as a choice type parses clean, and the document tokenizer then
ignores the declared type entirely, matching UID with its own hardcoded
expression. The declaration is a statement the grammar accepts and nothing
honours. The same holds for MID.

The operator's call was that this is not worth modelling in the Nix layer: the
reserved names are ones we depend on rather than ones to ban, and the surface
would be asserting a rule about a handful of names to catch a mistake nobody
has made. Recorded so that it reads as a decision rather than an oversight, and
so a later session does not spend the afternoon rediscovering it.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Five of this repository's ten field names are reserved, so the behaviour is not
exotic. The reason to leave it alone is proportion, not ignorance of it.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
